Unchecked climate change will reduce global GDP by 10–23% by 2100, according to a 2015 meta-analysis in Nature; acting now costs roughly 1–2% of GDP per year.
Extreme weather events — floods, droughts, wildfires, hurricanes — have caused $3.6 trillion in economic losses globally since 1970, with frequency and severity rising.
The World Bank estimates 216 million people could be forced to migrate within their own countries by 2050 due to climate impacts, creating the largest refugee crisis in history.
Low-income countries and communities, which contributed least to emissions, face the greatest risks: Bangladesh, the Sahel, and Pacific Island nations are already experiencing existential threats.
